Chapter: WrappingBandNarrow
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A wrapping band narrower than its hardware width is refused at parse.

 Until 2026-07-28 it was accepted and meant something else. `wrapping`
 is the mode that promises the modular arithmetic the machine already
 does, and the machine does it at the width of the store: a one-byte
 store wraps mod 256 whatever the band says. So `between 0 and 100
 wrapping` stored 200 and read 200 back, out of its own declared range.

 That is not a wrong answer to be shrugged at. The static bounds prover
 reads a field's declared range off the declaration and elides the
 runtime check for any value it can prove fits, so a field holding 200
 while the prover believes it holds at most 100 puts a false range under
 every elision downstream of it. The refusal closes that by construction
 rather than by a check that has to keep being right.

 The positive control is `ops/bounded-modes-smoke`, whose bands are
 exactly u8 and i8 and which still compiles and still wraps.

Section: Types

  Narrow = record {
   v : Integer between 0 and 100 wrapping
  }

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (show ((Narrow { v = __narrow 7 }).v))
  end
